|> Section
    title=The Kolmogorov three-series theorem
    handle=sec-k-3-series

    As a further application of the Lindeberg-Feller central limit theorem, we prove the Kolmogorov three-series theorem (Theorem >>thm-three-series).

    |> Proof
        alt-title=Proof of Theorem >>thm-three-series

        Denote $Y_n = X_n \mathbf{1}_{\{|X_n|\le 1\}}$. Assume that the eponymous three series
        $$
        \begin{align*}
        eq:k-first-series##<< &\sum_{n=1}^\infty \mathrm{P}(|X_n|>1) = \sum_{n=1}^\infty \mathrm{P}(X_n\neq Y_n), \\
        eq:k-second-series##<< &\sum_{n=1}^\infty \mathbf{E}(Y_n), \\
        eq:k-third-series##<< &\sum_{n=1}^\infty \mathbf{V}(Y_n)
        \end{align*}
        $$
        all converge. Applying Theorem >>thm-one-series to the random variables $Y_n-\mathbf{E}(Y_n)$, from the assumption that (>>eq:k-third-series) converges we infer that the series $\sum_{n=1}^\infty \big( Y_n-\mathbf{E}(Y_n) \big)$ converges almost surely. Since (>>eq:k-second-series) converges, this also means that $\sum_n Y_n$ converges almost surely. Since (>>eq:k-first-series) converges we get using the first Borel-Cantelli lemma that the event $\{ X_n \neq Y_n\textrm{ i.o.} \}$ has probability~$0$. Outside this event, the series $\sum_n X_n$ converges if and only if $\sum_n Y_n$ converges, so $\sum_n X_n$ also converges almost surely.

        Next, for the converse claim, assume that $\sum_n X_n$ converges with positive probability. By the Kolmogorov $0$-$1$ law, it therefore converges a.s. We need to show that the series (>>eq:k-first-series), (>>eq:k-second-series), (>>eq:k-third-series) converge.

        First, note that (>>eq:k-first-series) must converge, since if it doesn't, the second Borel-Cantelli lemma implies that the event $\{ |X_n|>1 \textrm{ i.o.} \}$ has probability one,which would imply that $\sum X_n$ diverges almost surely.

        Second, assume by contradiction that (>>eq:k-third-series) diverges, or equivalently that
        $$v_n := \sum_{k=1}^n \mathbf{V}(Y_n) \to \infty\ \textrm{ as }n\to\infty.$$
        We apply Theorem >>thm-lindeberg-feller to the triangular array
        $$ X_{n,k} = v_n^{-1/2} \left(Y_k - \mathbf{E} Y_k\right). $$
        Assumptions 1, 2 and 3 of the theorem are trivially satisfied (with $\sigma^2 = \sum_{k=1}^n \mathbf{V}(X_{n,k}) = 1$ for all $n$). For assumption 4, note that $|X_{n,k}| \le 2 v_n^{-1/2}$, so
        $X_{n,k}^2 \mathbf{1}_{\{|X_{n,k}|>\epsilon\}} = 0$ for all $k$ if $v_n > 4/\epsilon^2$, which holds for $n$ large enough. Thus, the assumptions of the theorem are valid and hence we obtain the conclusion that $S_n=\sum_{k=1}^n X_{n,k} \implies N(0,1)$ as $n\to\infty$.
        Define a sequence (of numbers, not random variables)
        $$
        t_n = v_n^{-1/2} \sum_{k=1}^n \mathbf{E} Y_k = S_n - v_n^{-1/2}\sum_{k=1}^n Y_k,
        $$
        and note that on the event $\{\sum_n X_n \textrm{ converges} \}$ (in which case clearly $\sum_n Y_n$ also converges), which we assumed has probability~1, $S_n-t_n = v_n^{-1/2} \sum_{k=1}^n Y_k \to 0$ as $n\to\infty$. This is easily seen to be in contradiction to the convergence in distribution of $S_n$ to a limiting distribution of a non-constant random variable. So we have shown that (>>eq:k-third-series) converges.

        Finally, having shown that (>>eq:k-third-series) converges, we conclude from Theorem >>thm-one-series that the series $\sum_n \left(Y_n-\mathbf{E}(Y_n)\right)$ converges almost surely. We already saw that on the event where $\{ \sum_n X_n \textrm{ converges}\}$, which we assumed has positive probability, also $\sum_n Y_n$ converges, and therefore the series $\sum_n \mathbf{E}(Y_n) = \sum \big(Y_n - (Y_n-\mathbf{E}(Y_n))\big)$ also converges.
